From aad10db6da5cd9cd750f77c709e5a5013f7b2941 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:30:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: map HDR10 and HLG to an SDR output with the
 bridge

With the tone mapping method of a reference display, an HDR10 or HLG
picture on an output that takes no HDR (an SDR display, the panel of a
laptop, a TV without HDR, or Kodi's HDR output turned off) is mapped to SDR
by the bridge, as a reconstructed profile 5 picture is (dvbridge_render_plain()
with dvbridge_sdr_mapping()). So is the base layer of a Dolby Vision stream
that is a picture by itself (profiles 7 and 8), which Kodi took to SDR as an
HDR10 one; never that of profile 5, which is reconstructed instead.

DVBRIDGE::PlainMappingEligible() says which: BT.2020 primaries, the BT.2020
matrix for non-constant luminance, and HLG, or PQ with a peak in its HDR10
metadata (the mastering display's white or MaxCLL; without either the curve
would guess, and the picture goes Kodi's way); not interlaced, not
stereoscopic; VAAPI pictures of 3840x2160 at most, as for the reconstruction.
The renderer sets the output up for an SDR picture of BT.709, takes the
mapping with the mastering display's primaries, and draws the picture through
the window system and the bridge into the window, at Kodi's place for it.

It fails closed: an output that takes HDR signalling for it, a picture the
bridge cannot map (the one before it is shown; where there is none, Kodi's
own Hable draws it) and, after 48 in a row, the rest of the video as Kodi
plays it. Changing the method during a video configures the renderer again.

Measured with the software bridge (dvbridge_render_plain(), zink on
lavapipe) against Kodi's methods written out from its GLES shaders, on an
SDR display of 100 cd/m2 (BT.1886), Delta E ITP (ITU-R BT.2124) against the
source's light with its reference white (203 cd/m2, ITU-R BT.2408) at the
display's white, where the display can show it so:

  - a PQ chart mastered to 4000 cd/m2, its patches of at most 5 cd/m2 (28):
    the bridge 0.12 on average (at most 2.2), Hable 14.2, Reinhard 18.9,
    ACES 13.7; a grey of 0.91 cd/m2 shown at 0.445 (ideal 0.446), Hable
    0.218, Reinhard 1.07, ACES 0.261;
  - the hue (ICtCp) of the ColorChecker with its white at 203 cd/m2: off by
    at most 1.3 degrees, Hable 4.2, Reinhard 4.8, ACES 8.8; of colours at the
    edge of BT.2020 at 800 cd/m2: 9.5, Hable 35.1, Reinhard 53.5, ACES 33.5;
  - an HLG chart, patches of at most 5 cd/m2: 0.06 against 15.6 for Kodi's
    way (no tone mapping, the primaries converted on the signal); its
    ColorChecker with white at 50 cd/m2: 0.74 against 26.5;
  - nine pictures of the base layer of the FEL test stream (HDR10, 1000
    cd/m2), their pixels of at most 5 cd/m2: median 0.01 to 0.13, Hable 4.5
    to 12.2, Reinhard 1.0 to 25.6, ACES 2.3 to 17.0; in the brightest
    picture the hue of 99 % of the pixels within 11.8 degrees, Hable 37.3,
    Reinhard 30.1, ACES 40.4.

What no measure settles: every way compresses the highlights, and how much is
a choice; the perceptual gamut mapping desaturates saturated colours near
the edge of BT.709 as well (in that brightest picture, its pixels of at most
30 cd/m2: median 15.8, Hable 16.3, ACES 12.4).

The rule is tested on the host (every combination of its ten conditions;
three mutations of it caught). The rest is compiled in syntax-only mode and
read; none of it has run in Kodi or on a display.
